fafd9e8-7e2c-5da3-8224-387ebb42b609" :question/math-academy-id "q-102605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/dcac814ed1c1caf5a8796a25ccee57cc5fd988d10f24503cbf0755aeaedb5e2c.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=8t,y=-{t}^{2}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8t)-(-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4t+{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-102605/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102605"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-320707/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer/id #uuid "6e2988ea-9a75-5853-b00e-c6e0cbc77514" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320707/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3b5dbe-3135-5f08-a051-0124a9af6380" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320707/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer-field/choices ["q-320707/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320707/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8" :answer/id #uuid "179d4564-b03d-5d49-a0b3-2a52ac0eab02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320707/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "85be5aad-d193-5242-aec3-d413f016943e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320707/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8" :answer-field/choices ["q-320707/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320707" :question/id #uuid "257853a6-f390-514e-8b43-63ff1af6b27b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320707" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $3y+x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-320707/field-1" "q-320707/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320707"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer/id #uuid "ce1f35c5-624a-55f6-b2d3-b812c9298f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer/id #uuid "901020d6-1243-5159-868a-53f40cac35a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "577205e3-b3b3-51bc-9a82-c1fb625e405b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320705/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-320705/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" "q-320705/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705/field-2/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3" :answer/id #uuid "4663ef2d-ba6c-5580-99e0-e19be4ff7414" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f15d0a15-084f-5fc7-a102-216c7bb1ddc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320705/field-2/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3" :answer-field/choices ["q-320705/field-2/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320705" :question/id #uuid "0f50a531-00f6-5973-a263-cf574ee4f075" :question/math-academy-id "q-320705" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-320705/field-1" "q-320705/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320705"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection/052e3f7b4ebb2e2e0bfbe53a5f861b31925ea3e62cadf4287aea5784a0eb48b0" :answer/id #uuid "4b46f4d0-8627-5b28-b4e7-7a8115e3c0b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection/b9cbc173ed21773c9bbf0f7485b72882f849d9fc08f51455e4768e3e1e0a0c4d" :answer/id #uuid "0e0cfbf3-a474-588a-a8f6-ee27ee7a5f3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection/ee453e223c033faaca082fa14c457192ee72533dd5179bf18bbc99456bf1312b" :answer/id #uuid "e39756fc-87b8-5d77-a1dd-db579bdb2113" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection/9a6845fe9655167fa816a92e29de5f0131f98bac45ee231b02f5dd4fb2ecfcb0" :answer/id #uuid "087c2bbd-ff5a-5c03-977e-db28acb32f72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection/62b7f1dc60b627fa5546997d66fea03e1aa82a651b3df9900023b40ea2c37d16" :answer/id #uuid "76cb81ba-521b-5e15-9011-60778fdf52e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cea939-28cb-56ad-b007-22bdaefef6a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-16178/selection/9a6845fe9655167fa816a92e29de5f0131f98bac45ee231b02f5dd4fb2ecfcb0" :answer-field/choices ["q-16178/selection/052e3f7b4ebb2e2e0bfbe53a5f861b31925ea3e62cadf4287aea5784a0eb48b0" "q-16178/selection/b9cbc173ed21773c9bbf0f7485b72882f849d9fc08f51455e4768e3e1e0a0c4d" "q-16178/selection/ee453e223c033faaca082fa14c457192ee72533dd5179bf18bbc99456bf1312b" "q-16178/selection/9a6845fe9655167fa816a92e29de5f0131f98bac45ee231b02f5dd4fb2ecfcb0" "q-16178/selection/62b7f1dc60b627fa5546997d66fea03e1aa82a651b3df9900023b40ea2c37d16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16178" :question/id #uuid "50a3f4ad-674b-5669-b9c0-40f8b988aec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16178" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16178/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16178"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer/id #uuid "cf184d60-bd1c-57ca-9a3a-8381d419ad06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer/id #uuid "5bee9285-247c-5822-bfed-7822d6a248b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc3504f-61bb-5e8a-9082-7896820b72b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320696/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer-field/choices ["q-320696/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" "q-320696/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696/field-2/0a6ddc2b5a7aa53dbaf860b36b1154868d90594d50efab51f97b669902110496" :answer/id #uuid "e6bd22f5-9c8f-5fd6-b483-cd0264c966f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c170e32b-02eb-503b-8a0d-9297e0ee75b8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-320696/field-2/0a6ddc2b5a7aa53dbaf860b36b1154868d90594d50efab51f97b669902110496" :answer-field/choices ["q-320696/field-2/0a6ddc2b5a7aa53dbaf860b36b1154868d90594d50efab51f97b669902110496"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-320696" :question/id #uuid "e216f63e-6792-5778-92c0-9dae49201579" :question/math-academy-id "q-320696" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-320696/field-1" "q-320696/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-320696"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection/d40db7029e85b755bed007fbe25a6cb6a204022299864d5c9abd226173a2a06e" :answer/id #uuid "c3a8564e-6ded-539c-b919-ba8b6e841361" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection/9325cfe997a50020734587f30cdc3ad3ae5bb7ff9edfe79d0122c7b949dcd0a3" :answer/id #uuid "b813a7bc-5d32-5a20-a398-4cf85e83e27d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection/935b688c4032aed1fd015f00d9dc84f487cdd173da562efa2333366397694dec" :answer/id #uuid "2bd57dc9-b855-5e9a-a6cc-c3de93d6fa06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection/872458ca118c4f6c0f6d8a05131e88a0b9a18eb5e94916f6336ae0c25bc50144" :answer/id #uuid "cca5c6f9-9ecc-5b70-9979-e6c98222f49a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection/e83e250f623e62a0efb1018ad568def367014664e11d658f12647750fe48063d" :answer/id #uuid "a4ca16a8-1284-50f7-a316-3444027c2f73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d108d3-928b-5080-b96f-b54581e22218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-102633/selection/e83e250f623e62a0efb1018ad568def367014664e11d658f12647750fe48063d" :answer-field/choices ["q-102633/selection/d40db7029e85b755bed007fbe25a6cb6a204022299864d5c9abd226173a2a06e" "q-102633/selection/9325cfe997a50020734587f30cdc3ad3ae5bb7ff9edfe79d0122c7b949dcd0a3" "q-102633/selection/935b688c4032aed1fd015f00d9dc84f487cdd173da562efa2333366397694dec" "q-102633/selection/872458ca118c4f6c0f6d8a05131e88a0b9a18eb5e94916f6336ae0c25bc50144" "q-102633/selection/e83e250f623e62a0efb1018ad568def367014664e11d658f12647750fe48063d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-102633" :question/id #uuid "74376924-ed55-5466-916c-abf65e0085f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102633" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $x-5y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5y & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-102633/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-102633"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5373"] :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/ba682be1ab26bad257fe814020469696f5c302a765a67c45c341762ac9418108.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y-15 & =0 \\\\ 4({t}^{2})-2(2t)-15 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t-15 & =0 \\\\ (2t-5)(2t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$  at the points $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5372"] :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,$ so our first solution is $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
